rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p1 rice:passageText """The disease was reported in Japan in 1911 and in Italy in 1912 by Yamada and Brizi (Ou 1985). It has also been observed on rice in Australia, northeastern and eastern mainland China, Taiwan, Arkansas USA, Korea (Jung et al 1974), and India (Dasgupta and Mukhaerjee 1976). Besides rice, the fungus has also been shown to attack a wide range of hosts, including more than 43 genera in the Gramineae family. The fungal pathogen can infect oats, barley, wheat, rye, millet, and maize (Akai and Fukutomi 1964)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p2 rice:passageText """The disease is known as “yellow wilt” in India (Dasgupta and Mukhaerjee 1976). It adversely affects the development of individual plants but not significantly rice yield, maybe due to its occurrence at low temperatures under field conditions. The active temperature range for disease development is 15-25oC with an optimum of about 20oC or slightly lower (Yamanaka and Kawai 1961). The disease, therefore, occurs only in a temperate rice production environment where the rice crop is likely to suffer stress due to low temperature."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p3 rice:passageText """In the earlier stage of the disease, new leaves of infected plants produce chlorotic whitish spots or patches on the leaf blades, sometimes reducing them in size. At times, fluffy white to grey fungal growth may be seen on the under surface of the leaf blades. Infected young shoots and seeds may also show white coating of fungal growth."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p4 rice:passageText """In severe cases, the leaves may be distorted or twisted. The symptoms are more pronounced at flowering time. The panicles appear irregular, partially emerged from the leaf sheath freely and become contorted and, sometimes, spiral (DM Figure 1). The leaf sheath may also be twisted. The emerged panicle is reduced in size with part of its floral parts modified into twisted tubular structures and usually remains green longer than normal."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p5 rice:passageText """The fungus was named by Brizi in 1912 (Ou 1985) as Sclerospora oryzae Brizi. In 1911, Yamada, following Saccardo’s description of the fungus, called it Sclerospora macrospora Sacc. (Ou 1985). Hara (1930) renamed it Phytophthora oryzae (Brizi) Hara based on the appearance of the sporangial stage that was more like that of Phytophthora than Sclerospora, while Tanaka and Ito (1940) believed that it should be P . macrospora (Sacc.) Ito & Tanaka. Thirumalachar et al (1953) later created a new genus, Sclerophthora, based on the sporangial stage that was similar to Phytophthora’s and the oogonial stage that was like Sclerospora’s. Today, the fungus that causes DM of rice is accepted by most scientists as Sclerophthora macrospore (Sacc.) Thirum, Shaw, and Naras."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p6 rice:passageText """Thirumalachar et al (1953) provided the description of the fungus morphology. The mycelium is hyaline and coenocytic; the sporangial stage Phytophthora-like; sporangiospores hyploid, very little differentiated from the hyphae within the host, simple or sympodially and successively branched; sporangia large, lemon-shaped, apical poroid, borne singly at the apices of the sporangiophores, germinating in water by the division of the cell contents into biciliate zoospores. The oogonial stage is similar to that of Sclerospora; oogonial wall thickened and confluent with the wall of the oospores. Oospore germination is indirect by sporangial formation. S. macrospore readily forms sporangia on an infected young leaf but not old leaves, which are less favorable for infection. High humidity, low temperature, and field flooding at the tillering stage are critical for DMto occur. However, light inhibits sporangia formation (Jee et al 2002, Nakagama et al 1995)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p7 rice:passageText """The measurement of the various structures of the fungus on rice is as follows (Tasugi 1953): sporangia, 60-114 x 28-57 µm (av. 83.1 x 42.2 µm); zoospores, 9-16 µm; cysospores, 9-15 µm; oogonia, 46-80 x 42-69 µm; antheridia, 20-48 x10-29 µm; oospores, 36-67 x32- 64 µm. This information is cited from Ou (1985). The oospores are initially hyaline, then become whitish yellow. Matured oospores form a yellowish line on both sides of the veins of leaf blades."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p8 rice:passageText """The fungus overwinters in infected rice leaves as oospores, which germinate to produce zoospores in early spring. When rice or wheat seeds germinate, the zoospores are attracted and attached to the plumule during the early stage of seed germination or infect wild grasses and rice seedlings in the nursery. After a short resting time, the zoospores produce germ tubes that penetrate into the first leaf (Akai Fukutomi 1964b, Gui 1980). Infection takes place through the epiblast or ventral scale and the hyphae are attracted to the growing point of the seedling. Oospores germinate in flooded fields in autumn but not in dry fields (Takatsu and Toyama 1957). Grasses in the vicinity of rice fields are considered an important source of primary inoculum for infecting rice seedlings in the seedbed (Katsura et al 1954)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p9 rice:passageText """The symptoms first appear on the second leaf. If migration or invasion of the hypha is into the parenchyma of the growing point, the plants will eventually degenerate. However, if invasion is into vascular strands, the plants will continue to grow and oospores will be formed (Yamanaka and Kawai 1964a)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p10 rice:passageText """The optimum temperature for infection is 18-20oC, at which the infection process is completed in 15-20 hours by the oospores (Katsura 1965). However, zoospore infection may be completed in only 3 hours (Yamanaka and Kawai 1964b). The disease occurs frequently in flooded fields. Oospores are rarely observed in the early stage of infection but are abundant at later infection stage."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p11 rice:passageText """2.3.1. Disease diagnosis. The rice DM syndrome includes dwarfing, yellowing, malformation, excessive tillering, and whitish to yellowish spots on the leaf blades that are somewhat similar to some virus diseases. Its diagnosis in the field, particularly during vegetative stage of the crop, is often difficult. However, at the ear head stage, the fully or partially modified inflorescence can be conspicuously seen. Sporangial formation in the laboratory may facilitate diagnosis (Jee et al 2002)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p12 rice:passageText """The following procedure may be useful in diagnosis. • Cut fresh young leaves showing whitish or yellowish spots on leaf blades coupled with other associated symptoms into 2- to 3-cm sections. • Wash the leaf sections (about 10-20 pieces) vigorously in 70% ethanol for 30 seconds; then rinse three times in distilled water. • Submerge the leaf sections in 10 ml of Millipored filtered paddy water in a 100-ml Duran bottle; incubate at 20°C in the dark for 8-10 hr. • Agitate the bottle vigorously in a Vortex mixer. • Spread 0.1-1.0 ml of the suspension on glass slides and examine for oospores under microscope at 50- or 100-X magnification."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_downy-mildew_p13 rice:passageText """The disease does not warrant special control measures, as its optimum temperature range for occurrence is at the lower end of rice growing condition even in temperate environment. Thus, one would not expect this disease to occur regularly on rice crop production. However, because of climate change, it is still necessary to monitor its occurrence especially during sudden change in temperature during early stage of rice crop growth."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p1 rice:passageText """As early as 1955, bacterial GnR was observed in Kyushu, Japan (Goto and Ohata 1956). Subsequently, it has been reported in Taiwan (Chien et al 1963), Korea in 1995 (Cha 1993, Song and Hyung Moo 1999), and mainland China in 2007 (Luo et al 2007). In the southern United States, bacterial panicle blight similar to grain rot had been reported in the early 1990s (Nandakumar et al 2009). Initially, the symptoms were thought to be caused by an abiotic stress related to high temperature, water, or toxic chemicals. The malady was later confirmed to be of bacterial origin caused by B. glumae and B. gladioli and identical to grain rot reported in Japan (Shahjahan et al 2000). It is then possible that other disease problems exist that may be attributed to nonbiological factors. In the United States, the disease is called bacterial panicle blight but in other countries the disease is known as grain rot."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p2 rice:passageText """In Japan, GnR was initially regarded as a minor problem. However, since the 1970s, the incidence and severity continued to intensify in southern and central Japan (Uematsu et al 1976a,b), which coincided with the time when the conventional field nursery had been replaced by the nursery box for raising seedlings. In mid-1970s, the first incidence of B. glumae infecting seedlings in a nursery box was observed, showing that the bacterial pathogen attacks rice both at the seedling and panicle development stages (Uematsu et al 1976a,b). The importance of B. glumae as a rice bacterial pathogen has since been recognized. B. glumae has since been manifested as grain rot of mature plants at the heading stage in the field and as seedling rot in nursery boxes. However, seedling rot seldom occurs in field nursery. While examining the bacterial species associated with grain or sheath rot in a large number of seed samples from 22 different countries, Zeigler and Alvarez (1990) noted that Pseudomonas glumae (syn. B. glumae) among the four frequently encountered bacterial seed pathogens with a worldwide distribution."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p3 rice:passageText """The disease manifests itself into distinct types of symptoms on rice plants (Sayler et al 2006; Uematsu et al 1976a,b). At the seedling stage in a nursery box, it is seedling rot while on grains in fields, it is grain rot. On leaf blades, especially flag leaves, the disease appears as small tan lesions measuring 1 to 5 mm with brown margins. On the leaf sheath, the lesion may be several centimeters long with a tan center and reddish brown margins on individual flag leaf sheaths of infected tillers. Infection occurs on spikelets causing kernel abortion before the kernels are completely filled. Infected florets may show dark gray or brown bases, but the rachis of the panicles remains green. GnR is usually realized when the infected seeds are sown for germination in nursery boxes. Panicles of infected tillers remain erect instead of bending due to loss of grain weight."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p4 rice:passageText """The infection of seedlings results in seedling rot, likely derived from seedborne inoculum. Seed infestation or infection increases during presowing and water soaking or when seedlots carry the bacterial inoculum. Preemergence “damping off” causes GnR where the seeds fail to germinate and also forms a source of inoculum for other germinated seeds of disease foci in nursery boxes. The symptoms on seedlings caused by inoculum from rotting seeds usually develop into brown lesions on the coleoptile (Uematsu et al 1976a,b), which can cause seedling mortality. Lesions on infected and developed seedlings show brown, water-soaked soft rot of the leaf sheath accompanied by wilting or soft rot of the leaves (GnR Figure 1)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p5 rice:passageText """On grain rot, the panicles are shrunken and pale green, becoming dirty yellow to brown and dry. The symptoms may appear on the glumes and kernels. However, in Japan, panicle rachises and branches do not show symptoms. The hulls of infected grains on young panicles rapidly fade in color from the base, turning greenish white at first and then pale pink to yellowish brown. They soon wither and dry up. The infected grains are either sterile or carry brown rice with dark-brown discoloration at the base. A brown margin between the infected and healthy parts of the grain is a diagnostic feature of the disease (GnR Figure 2). The bacterium may cause mild rot of the flag leaf sheath of the leaf sheath collar."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p6 rice:passageText """GnR is caused by Burkholderia glumae (formerly Pseudomonas glumae Kurita et Tabei 1967). The bacterium is a gram-negative, nonspore-forming rod, measuring 0.5-1.0 x 1.5-3.0 µm, and with round ends. The cells occur singly, rarely in pairs, and are mobile by means of one or several polar flagella. It forms white colonies on nutrient agar. A fluorescent pigment is also produced on potato agar (Urakami et al 1994). Granules of poly-β-hydroxybutyrate are accumulated in the cells. The bacterium grows well at 30oC and at 37oC but not at 42oC. The optimum pH for bacterial growth is between pH 5.0 and 7.5, but not at a pH value below 3.0 or above 9.5. Strains of B. glumae produce crystals of calcium oxalate on agar medium containing calcium chloride. The genetic diversity of B. glumae isolates is relatively low as revealed by 16S-23S rDNA ITS sequence analysis (Sayler et al 2006)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p7 rice:passageText """Natural infection of the bacterial pathogen has been reported on rice. When artificially inoculated with the pathogen, several plant species were weakly infected but their role in the life cycle of the bacterium remains insignificant."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p8 rice:passageText """B. glumae is seedborne and epiphytic on rice plants. The bacterium invades the glumes through the stomata located outside the lemmas colonizing the space between the cells in the outer epidermis and spongy parenchyma of the lemma. From the infected seeds, the bacterium may grow epiphytically at a low level until panicle emergence when its population increases rapidly on the grain. The bacterium multiplies rapidly on the surface of emerging panicles, and infect flowers just after emergence (Goto 1992). The disease is favored by high night temperature (28oC) and high humidity."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p9 rice:passageText """1.4.1. Toxoflavin, a phytotoxin produced by B. glumae. On various media, B. glumae produces bright pigments identified as the phytotoxins toxoflavin (1,6-dimethylpyrimidio[5,4-e]-1,2,4-triazine-5,7(1H,6H)-dione), a 7-azapteridine antibiotic, and fervenulin in culture. Both toxins produce chlorosis and reduce the growth of leaves and roots (Sato et al 1989; Liyama et al 1994,1995). Toxoflavin-induced wilt symptoms in other field crops such as tomato, sesame, and perilla indicate the general broad spectrum of this toxin (Jeong et al 2003). Toxoflavin induces a wilt symptom in the field on crop species other than rice. This symptom on other crop species is similar to that caused by Ralstonia solanacearum. Phytotoxins have been reported in many pseudomonad bacteria but none has been implicated in pathogenicity. Toxoflavin is one of the few shown to be involved in pathogenicity and its requirement for quorum sensing for pathogenicity is the first report for plant pathogenic bacteria."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p10 rice:passageText """1.4.2. Detection of B. glumae from rice seed. B. glumae is seedborne and can be detected from rice seeds. It can also be selectively isolated from rice seed using a selective medium described by Tsushima et al (1986) where B. glumae produces circular, convex colonies with smooth margins that are reddish brown or opalescent with a purple or red-purple center. However, this technique may not be accurate for plant quarantine purposes."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p11 rice:passageText """As it is a seedborne disease, GnR was listed as a plant quarantine disease in China in 2007. Attempts have been made to devise sensitive methods of detection using molecular tools. A real-time PCR method was developed to detect and identify the bacterium from rice seed using specific PCR primers based on the sequences of internal transcribed spacer (ITS) region of the 16S-23S rDNA of representative isolates from Japan and Arkansas, USA (Sayler et al 2006). Yuan et al (2009) developed a real-time PCR and a general PCR diagnostic method for its detection in seedlots. A 319-bp fragment specific to B. glumae produced in both these methods confirms the presence and identity of the pathogen."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p12 rice:passageText """Options to control GnR are limited to using clean seed and resistant varieties.

1.5.1. Seed health approach using clean seed. For seedborne rice diseases, such as those reported here, seed health management may be one of the most preferred control practices. Healthy seed must be used for planting. Seeds from fields known to harbor grain rot must be avoided. Infected seeds can easily be recognized because these are generally lighter in weight although research on this may not be adequate. For field bulk seed planting, seeds may be suspended in salt water to sort out the best quality seeds for planting (Goto 1992). This is an old method but remains one of the most effective means to manage seedborne rice diseases. The various approaches to seed health are described in detail in Section 2 of Part III, Selected Management Practices."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p13 rice:passageText """1.5.2. Varietal resistance. If resistance sources are available, deployment of resistant varieties is another option in managing grain rot. However, there is meager research on host plant resistance on grain rot. The long grain rice varieties, mostly indica types, are more tolerant to grain rot or panicle blight than medium to short grain types (Sayler et al 2006). There is no data to support any genetic basis on the variable responses of these two types of rice to the pathogen. Nevertheless, based on bacterial multiplication in leaf sheath at the 4th day after inoculation by injection, and based on lesion size on seedlings after spray inoculation, variations among tested varieties were evident (GnR Figure 3; Sayler et al 2006). There is still a lot to understand on the genetics of resistance to grain rot and in developing grain rot-resistant rice varieties. There is also the need to elucidate the role of seedling infection and bacterial multiplication and movement as epiphyte on the plant till panicle initiation or boot leaf stage to get an opportunity of infecting the florets for grain rot development."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p14 rice:passageText """1.5.3. Chemical control. There are several chemicals known to be effective in controlling GnR, especially for seedlings raised using seed boxes. However, proper guidance is necessary for their use."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_grain-rot_p15 rice:passageText """Several biological control agents (BCAs) have been discovered that are effective against the bacterial pathogen (Sumida and Takaya 2002). A general procedure for raising seedlings in nursery boxes is to presoak the seeds overnight before sowing. No research has shown whether BCAs can be formulated for large-scale seed-soaking and commercialized. However, there appears to be a good opportunity to formulate BCAs that can be used in conjunction with seed-soaking to suppress seedborne inocula. For details, see Section 3 on Managing microbial antagonists for rice disease control in Part III, Selected rice disease management practices."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p1 rice:passageText """The disease has three different names: leaf tip drying in China by Wei (1934), who was the first to describe the disease; leaf tip blight by Inoue and Takeda (1957), who suspected the causal organism to be the same agent of brown leaf blight; and LSc, given by Hashioka and Ikegami (1955)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p2 rice:passageText """LSc is common on rice plants in Latin America (De Gutierrez 1960), and Asia. It has also been reported in the USA (Rush 1973) and West Africa (Lamey and Williams 1972). The disease had caused considerable losses in Latin America and was “sometimes as bad as rice blast” in West Africa (Lamey and Williams 1972)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p3 rice:passageText """In tropical Asia, LSc sometimes occurs on rice leaves concomitantly with bacterial blight caused by Xanthomonas oryzae pv. oryzae. This appears to indicate that infection may be predisposed by wounds or the fungal pathogen invades the host tissue via injury or necrotic tissues. Thus, leaf clipping appears to be an effective method of producing the disease (Ou et al 1978)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p4 rice:passageText """The lesions are characterized by zonation starting from the leaf tip or edges (Hashioka and Ikegami 1955), and usually occur on mature leaves. They are more or less oblong with light brown halos. Individual lesions are 1-5 cm long, 0.5-1 cm wide. The continuous enlargement and coalescing of lesions may result in blight on a large part of the leaf blade; lesions as long as 25 cm have been observed. The zonation fades as the lesions become old. Lesion without distinct zonation is called leaf tip blight in Japan, and leaf tip drying in China (Ou 1985) (LSc Figure 1)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p5 rice:passageText """In Japan, brown leaf blight typically shows small, scattered, reddish-brown spots without necrotic tissues and with an indistinct margin. Leaf sheath browning is characterized by relatively large patches of brown lesions without necrosis and indistinct margins. Both types of symptoms are only occasionally observed in the tropics. The symptoms on the leaf sheath are easily confused with those of other diseases."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p6 rice:passageText """In Korea, besides the typical leaf symptoms, small, reddish-brown spots on the leaves (brown leaf blight) and long, elliptical or rectangular, purplish-brown or light grey were also described (Kwon et al 1973). Lesions on the neck of the panicles, similar to those on the leaf sheath, have also been observed (Kwon et al 1973). Von Thϋmen (cf. Wei 1934) indicated that the grains were infected. De Gutierrez (1960) reported a head blight causing considerable sterility, flower deformation and discoloration of glumes in Costa Rica. She described the fungus as causing decay of coleoptiles with red brown infection and root rot."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p7 rice:passageText """Wei (1934) reported that a leaf tip drying disease was caused by an ascomycete, Metqasphaeria albescens Thϋmen, but did not give a complete description of the organism. Inoue and Takeda (1957) and Koshimizu et al (1970) reported the causal agent to be Phragmosperma Theiss. & Syd. Hashioka and Ikegami (1955) described brown leaf blight as a separate disease because of the specific zonate lesions. Based on pathogenicity testing, Kitani and Ohata (1969) indicated that the causal fungi of brown leaf blight and LSc were identical. Hashioka and Ikegami (1955) identified the fungus as Rhynchosporium oryzae Hashioka & Yokogi, the anamorph state of the fungus. It was later renamed and transferred to Gerlachia oryza (Hashioka & Yokogi) W. Gams by Gams and Müller (1980) because the fungus shows no similarity with other species of Rhynchosporium sp. except its conidia (LSc Figure 2)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p8 rice:passageText """Conidia are predominantly bitunicate on the leaf surface borne from superficial stomata arising from the lesions; the conidiogenous structures emerge from the stomata. They are bow to new-moon shaped, single-celled when young, and 2-celled when mature, occasionally 2- or 3-septate, not constricted at the septum, epispore very thin, pink in mass and hyaline under the microscope. Ou et al (1978) averaged the different measurements of conidia as follows: 9-14 x 3-4.5 µm, mostly 10-12 x 3.5-4 µm."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p9 rice:passageText """Wei (1934) identified the teleomorph of the LSc fungus as Metasphaeria albescens Thümen and he also provided a full description on the conidial and on the ascogenous state of the fungus. According to Ou (1985), most workers apparently were not aware of Wei’s publication. Consequently, Inoue and Takeda (1957) suggested that the teleomorph belonged to the genus Phragmosperma, while Tominaga (1970) considered the anamorph was Fusarium nivale Ces. Ex Sacc. Matzuyama and Wakimoto (1977) compared the esterase and catalase zymograms of several isolates of F . nivale from different hosts and indicated that the rice LSc fungus was different from F . nivale. Ou et al (1978) found the teleomorph of the LSc fungus in the Philippines and, based on its morphology and growth temperature, suggested that the fungus was similar to Metasphaeria albescens Thümen. Parkinson et al (1981) transferred it to Monographella albescens (Thümen) Parkinson, Sivanesan & Booth because of its unitunicate asci while Metasphaeria Sacc. is a bitunicate loculoascomycete genus."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p10 rice:passageText """The description and measurements of the teleomorph of the LSc fungus are provided by Ou et al (1978) as follows. Perthecia embedded in leaf tissues, spherical or slightly depressed, dark brown with an ostiole, 50-180 x 40-170 µm, mostly 100-140 x 80-120 µm; asci cylindrical to club-shaped, often slightly curved, 8-spored, 40-65 x 10-14 µm; ascopores obliquely uniseriate to biseriate, elliptical or fusoid, colorless, 3-sepate, occasionally 4-septate, 10-25 x 3-6 um; paraphyses long and slender, colorless."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p11 rice:passageText """The formation and maturation of perithecia depends on the environment. If the diseased tissues dry rapidly, the perithecia are either not formed or will not mature. The teleomorph is produced in vitro by mating single ascospore culture on Sach’s medium with rice leaves (Crill et al 1979). The fungus appears to be homothallic."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p12 rice:passageText """Besides Oryza sativa, Echinochloa crus-galli is also reported to be a host of the fungus (Singh and Gupta 1980)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p13 rice:passageText """There is a lack of experimental information on how LSc is spread in the field. There is also no information on the source of secondary inoculum, thus of secondary infection in the field."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p14 rice:passageText """6.4.1. Primary inoculum. Gerlachia oryza is primarily a foliar pathogen in which the conidia are transmitted by water-splash and germinate on the leaf surface (Singh and Gupta 1983). Host tissue invasion usually begins at the leaf tips and margins (Ou 1985, Turner and Black 2001). The primary source of inoculum is from plant debris left in the field after harvest. The fungus is known to be seedborne (Mew and Gonzales 2002, Mia et al 1987) and, together with infected plant debris, rice seeds may serve as a source of inoculum. The transmission rate of the seedborne inoculum is affected by soil conditions (Mia and Safeeulla 1986). However, some report that the seedborne inoculum may play only a minimal role in transmitting the pathogen based on evidence that seeds collected from heavily infected field failed to initiate any LSc after sowing (Turner and Black 2001). Although seedborne inoculum may not play an immediate role in disease establishment in a field experiment, it does not suggest that it has no role in distance transmission during seed movement."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p15 rice:passageText """6.4.2. The infection process. A more detailed infection process was provided by Turner and Black (2001). Conidia inoculated on rice leaves first fuse or get connected with each other by germ tubes or short projections to form a conidial complex. Anastomosis of germ tubes from different conidia seems to be common and an important phenomenon in causing infection (Ou 1985). Infection derived from germ tubes through anastomosis is more likely to succeed than that without passing through such process. In contact with stomata, vigorous growth of the germ tubes is observed to produce appressorium-like structures of various sizes. From these structures, the infection hyphae develop, passing through the stomata slits and swelling in the substomatal cavities. Once the germ tube complex has penetrated the leaf tissues, the substomatal hyphae grow profusely, ramify into the intercellular spaces, and then into the mesophyll cells, and a network of mycelium develops (LSc Figure 3). Behind the lesions, a network of complex interconnected hyphae develops with no orientation (Turner and Black 2001). Ramification begins ahead of the expanding necrotic zone and thereafter the infected leaf tissues die (Turner and Black 2001)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p16 rice:passageText """In the necrotic zone, perithecia are formed, within which asci and ascospores are produced (LSc Figure 4; Turner and Black 2001). Again, it is unclear how and under what conditions the ascospores are discharged from the dead leaf tissue. More importantly, it is assumed that these ascospores will continue the initial infection. On artificial inoculation, however, short, branched conidiophores grow out through the stomata and produce spore masses about 3 days after the inoculation. Penetration through stomata appears to be the likely step to complete the life cycle of the fungus and thus may appear to be a more frequent method of infection. The fungal pathogen is also capable of direct penetration of rice leaf tissue (Turner and Black 2001). It is often observed in the field that LSc lesions develop into bacterial blight lesions such as those produced by artificial inoculation with X. oryzae pv. oryzae using the leaf clipping method, which seems to suggest that G. oryzae is more capable of invading injured rice leaves than normal leaf tissues. LSc is also observed on leaves with blast lesions (Turner and Black 2001)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p17 rice:passageText """Lesion development is influenced by leaf wetness (Faria and Prabhu 1980). In a greenhouse experiment, in which inoculated plants were immediately removed from the dew chamber, no further disease development was observed (Faria and Prabhu 1980). The necrotic zonation becomes a demarcation of lesion advancement when the plant is under intermittent favorable and unfavorable moisture conditions during lesion development."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p18 rice:passageText """The general aspects of host plant resistance against foliar diseases have been discussed in relation to rice blast disease. Go to that section in this online resource for general information. Specific details or research conducted in relation to LSc are discussed below. In general, there is little work thus far done on resistance to this disease."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p19 rice:passageText """6.5.1. Inoculation techniques. Different inoculation techniques have been developed to test and evaluate varietal resistance against G. oryzae. The leaf clipping method originally devised for mass screening of rice varieties or breeding materials for resistance to bacterial blight seems to be most effective (LSc Table 1)."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p20 rice:passageText """Other methods to evaluate varietal resistance against G. oryzae have also been developed. Rice plants were grown in soil in plastic trays in a greenhouse for 30 days, after which the top two fully expanded leaves were inoculated by placing agar discs with mycelium of G. oryzae on the leaf surface (Faria and Prabhu 1980). The inoculated plants were then incubated in a dew chamber at 21-25oC. Relative lesion lengths among the rice genotypes tested are considered to express the level of susceptibility or resistance."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p21 rice:passageText """A detached leaf method was also developed and tested in assessing resistance (Bonman et al 1990). Lesion growth on detached and attached leaves was compared using different rice varieties. The detached leaf method gave results comparable to those of tests using intact plants. However, when the detached leaf method is used on a necrotrophic pathogen, take caution both in inoculation and in disease assessment as the leaf pieces can become senescent very rapidly. This can hamper the assessment among varieties for true resistance. A whole plant inoculation is required to evaluate actual resistance under field conditions. The in vitro method is sufficient for effective and efficient assessment of the general response of a large number of varieties before engaging in field evaluation."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p22 rice:passageText """6.5.2. Varietal resistance. Varietal difference is determined by testing a large number of rice accessions against isolates of the LSc pathogen. No specific research on host-pathogen interactions, mechanism for host resistance, and genetic basis for the resistance has been done. Available information indicates that resistance to M. albescens may be either nonspecific, where the reaction of the tested varieties against the pathogen isolates ranges from low to a high level of resistance (Bonman et al 1990), or a distinct response of the host genotypes to the virulence of the isolates (Turner and Black 2001). Evidently, understanding of resistance to LSc needs further research both on the disease development process and on the pathogen virulence to improve understanding on resistance and breeding."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p23 rice:passageText """6.5.3. Pathogen specialization. Diverse cultural characteristics have been observed on the fungus. Variations in conidial production were also noted among isolates when grown in liquid media (Singh and Gupta 1985). Based on the morphology of M. albescens in liquid culture media, Parkinson (1980) distinguished four main groups of the pathogen based on the abundance of aerial hyphae, conidial mass produced, and intensity of the mycelial pigmentation. However, in vitro fungal morphology should be treated cautiously when the information is used to relate to virulence (Turner and Black 2001). Turner and Black (2001) recognized distinctive patterns of host-pathogen interactions whenthey tested isolates from different geographical regions on a number of rice varieties (LSc Figure 5). Isolates 20.13 and 20.20 were virulent on rice variety ITA 306 while isolate 20.19 showed moderate virulence on this variety. Isolates 20.13 and 20.19 were virulent while isolate 20.20 seemed to be less virulent on variety SIPI. Although Turner and Black (2001) showed little information on host-pathogen interaction, the study did suggest possible physiologic specialization of the LSc fungus on different rice genotypes."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p24 rice:passageText """The design of effective disease control measures must be based on good knowledge of the epidemic process. In LSc, both seed and infected plant debris have been postulated to be potential sources of primary inoculum. For disease control, the disease can be minimized by using clean seeds and by field sanitation."""@en .
rice:IRRI_RD2018_leaf-scald_p25 rice:passageText """Although application of soluble silicon (Si) to indica rice varieties seemed to reduce the disease severity (Winslow 1992), similar effects were not observed when applied to a japonica type of upland rice. Although the level of Si in the japonica leaves was 100% higher than that in the leaves of indica lowland rice, there was no reduction on LSc in the latter (Turner and Black 2001). The results suggest that resistance to LSc has little to do with Si in the leaf tissues or that the effect of applied Si is dependent on the type of rice varieties and field conditions of rice cultivation. Thus, rice LSc may bear little similarity to rice blast or brown spot in which Si appears to be effective. For the effects of Si on rice blast and brown spot, go to the section on rice blast."""@en .
